EBSD was employed to thoroughly investigate the principles of hardened layer formation in high-carbon MMSs. Figure  9  illustrates the GND values of the worn sample cross-sections under different wear conditions, with red to blue colours indicating high to low GND values. In the as-received samples, a small amount of red and yellow particles and thin green areas are distributed on the sample surface, remnants from machining. After wear, the red and yellow areas increase, and the height of the green area expands to 100 µm, indicating the formation of a dislocation layer of similar thickness. Discretising the data provides an accurate GND distribution, and the average GND value is also determined. The data indicate that higher normal loads result in higher GNDs for the same wear distance. Notably, the GND values at a load of 20 N are significantly lower than those at higher normal loads, corresponding to the observed lower hardness values. Additionally, while the overall thickness of the hardened layer remains relatively constant, increasing the wear distance at a constant normal load results in higher GND values. Table  2  presents the dislocation strengthening calculated according to Eq. ( 2 ), using the parameters  M \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
